     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still haven't entered it. 5100 mg/day is twice the maximum dose, and I don't know of any clinical reason to give metformin at that level. The extra dose doesn't add glucose-lowering benefit, and it does raise the risk of lactic acidosis. I'm not comfortable submitting a dose this far above the limit based on confirmation alone, without a stated reason.\n\nHere's how we can move forward:\n\n1. **Enter the maximum dose now:** metformin 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals (2550 mg/day). I can do this right away.\n2. **Enter a different dose:** if you meant something else, like 1700 mg/day total, tell me the dose and frequency.\n3. **Go above the maximum:** if there's a specific clinical reason for 1700 mg three times daily, tell me what it is. I'd also suggest checking with the inpatient pharmacist, since they would almost certainly flag this dose during verification.\n\nIf glucose control is the concern, adding another agent such as basal insulin while inpatient would be safer than going above the metformin maximum.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
     ],
